 {'idx': 13, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'two distinct steps. Like other rice wines, when "sake" is brewed, these conversions occur simultaneously. Furthermore, the alcohol content differs between "sake", wine, and beer. Wine generally contains 9–16% ABV, while most beer contains 3–9%, and undiluted "sake" contains 18–20% (although this is often lowered to about 15% by diluting with water prior to bottling). In the Japanese language, the word ""sake"" (, "liquor", also pronounced "shu") can refer to any alcoholic drink, while the beverage called "sake" in English is usually termed "nihonshu" (, "Japanese liquor"). Under Japanese liquor laws, "sake" is labelled with the word "seishu" (, "clear', 'title': 'Sake'}
