 {'idx': 28, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': '"c" is specific heat capacity at constant pressure, "T" is temperature and "t" is time), and formula_2 is the energy conversion to and from thermal energy ("i" and "j" are for principal energy carriers). So, the terms represent energy transport, storage and transformation. Heat flux vector q is composed of three macroscopic fundamental modes, which are conduction (q = -"k"∇"T", "k": thermal conductivity), convection (q = "ρcuT", u: velocity), and radiation (q = formula_3s "I" sin"θdθdω", "ω": angular frequency, "θ": polar angle, "I": spectral, directional radiation intensity, s: unit vector), i.e., q = q + q + q. Once states', 'title': 'Heat transfer physics'}
