 {'idx': 2, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'The Walking Man The Walking Man () is a bronze sculpture by the French sculptor Auguste Rodin. It was created by Rodin during 1877 and 1878. The best example of Rodin’s ‘sketchy’ impressionist sculpture also happens to be his most well-known ‘incomplete’ figure. This work personifies the latter part of Rodin’s career: the dynamic pose of a partial figure. Deriving much from Rodin’s earlier work "St. John the Baptist Preaching", including the powerful stance, Rodin had stripped all academic associations from his figure, and instead focused on what he considered essential: the dynamic pose. According to the bibliography supplied by', 'title': 'The Walking Man'}
