[{'idx': 0, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'He also wrote the book "Mrs. Parkinson\'s Law: and Other Studies in Domestic Science". Parkinson\'s law Parkinson\'s law is the adage that "work expands so as to fill the time available for its completion". It is sometimes applied to the growth of bureaucracy in an organization. This law is likely derived from ideal gas law, whereby a gas expands to fit the volume allotted. Articulated by Cyril Northcote Parkinson as part of the first sentence of an essay published in "The Economist" in 1955 and since republished online, it was reprinted with other essays in the book "Parkinson\'s Law: The', 'title': "Parkinson's law"}
