 {'idx': 38, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'States (more widely in New York City than anywhere else across the continental U.S.). A similar product, with or without a chocolate coating, is sold as sponge candy in the United States. During manufacture, the honeycomb toffee is produced in large slabs, and is cut up using a highly focused jet of oil. The use of a blade would lead to fragmentation, while water would dissolve the honeycomb toffee. Oil prevents both of these happening, and produces uniform sharp-edged portions. The honeycomb toffee is then covered with chocolate, cooled, and packaged. In 2000, a short-lived (but successful) sister chocolate bar', 'title': 'Crunchie'}
