 {'idx': 9, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'cities, the custom of granting city status was briefly revived. The last grant of city status in the Netherlands was to Delfshaven in 1825. But the city status granted during this period was quite different from the privileges bestowed in the Middle Ages, and were merely symbolic. This is also the case for cities such as The Hague and Assen, which received their status during the Napoleonic period. City rights in the Low Countries City rights are a feature of the medieval history of the Low Countries. A liege lord, usually a count, duke or similar member of the high', 'title': 'City rights in the Low Countries'}]

