 {'idx': 12, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'fanciful or fantastic object". The phrase was deliberately absurd as a goose would never wear a bridle. Folk etymology converted the word "whim-wham"—a word that was no longer much used—to "wigwam", an Ojibwa word for a domed single-room dwelling used by Native Americans. This change retained the phrase\'s absurd meaning and sense. In Australia, a common usage is in response to an inquiry such as Q. "What are you making?", A. "A wigwam for a goose\'s bridle". The rejoinder was a code for "Mind your own business" and children acquired this pragmatic knowledge after repeated discourse with their parents ended', 'title': "A wigwam for a goose's bridle"}
