 {'idx': 14, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'to set a new world record of 47.84, slashing 0.34 seconds off the mark set by Klim from the relay. One of the most popular highlights in the event took place in the first heat. Dubbed as "Eric the Eel", Equatorial Guinea\'s Eric Moussambani received a dubious honor of being the slowest Olympic swimmer in history. Two other swimmers, Niger\'s Karim Bare and Tajikistan\'s Farkhod Oripov, plunged into the pool and were cast out of the race under a no false-start rule, leaving Moussambani as the last man standing. Cheering by a large crowd, he finished a one-man heat in', 'title': "Swimming at the 2000 Summer Olympics – Men's 100 metre freestyle"}
 {'idx': 15, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'Farkhod Oripov Farkhod Oripov (; born April 9, 1984) is a Tajik former swimmer, who specialized in sprint freestyle events. Oripov qualified for the men\'s 100 m freestyle at the 2000 Summer Olympics in Sydney, by receiving a Universality place from FINA without meeting a standard entry time. He participated in an unprecedented first heat against two other swimmers Karim Bare of Niger and Eric Moussambani of Equatorial Guinea, later dubbed himself as "Eric the Eel". Before the race began, Oripov plunged into the pool, along with Bare, beating the gun, and were eventually disqualified for a false start, leaving', 'title': 'Farkhod Oripov'}
